#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2520a3-f463-5681-90f0-2f2104ddbb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350758} {:answer/id #uuid "41bbb2d4-9959-5a3f-83fc-da1644e18d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350759} {:answer/id #uuid "504cdae1-8078-53e3-8c42-8480b7b0e7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350760} {:answer/id #uuid "2b021777-de03-5c6a-b397-99df22da23d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350761} {:answer/id #uuid "3fae0557-7c06-5501-bad6-e90c6ea1dd24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fae0557-7c06-5501-bad6-e90c6ea1dd24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350762} :answer-field/id #uuid "6054114f-e2e5-5444-bc00-736558a64725" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "787d93a7-5fc8-5bf3-9f23-708cb4e4f81a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106072" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transform the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}-y=0\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2} & =y \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a0db9e2-263a-5233-a6aa-d5225631746f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350765} {:answer/id #uuid "2060584b-7030-5f93-bee2-7376c2914474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350766} {:answer/id #uuid "f9bf24f1-5120-59d2-b918-c9a9bb3deacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350767} {:answer/id #uuid "ef62511a-4f0d-52b9-86b2-a801ef3bd39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350768} {:answer/id #uuid "57449b9a-4aac-510f-bdf1-15ed4844eee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef62511a-4f0d-52b9-86b2-a801ef3bd39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350768} :answer-field/id #uuid "27cfa124-d0b0-5893-8d89-a55349a6c896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0647dc2a-05fe-5a08-9885-3f888f72743b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109018" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{4}y=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{4},\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{4}{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{4}z=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a49fedd-ffc5-512b-a805-a87508b534c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350772} {:answer/id #uuid "047f16b4-7eaa-5deb-b03e-e7f2bfd251e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350773} {:answer/id #uuid "de6d00e8-a7fb-5dd9-88c5-e66ada7b017d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350774} {:answer/id #uuid "c27c3218-958a-567e-bbe0-91ee0724c7fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350775} {:answer/id #uuid "16832318-4eeb-5919-b0eb-7d0f7bd10403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a49fedd-ffc5-512b-a805-a87508b534c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350772} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e28f49-279e-59d6-a4f2-1bc08558956e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c44829d6-0133-55cb-8a5c-a20e996f6c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-109022" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}\\,z={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}\\,z=\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be25130b-1c34-58ab-b815-bac7386e26a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{x}{C{e}^{x/2}-1})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350779} {:answer/id #uuid "1146dd13-e9b9-532b-aa90-8e065391ffb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{C{e}^{2x}+x+1}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350780} {:answer/id #uuid "5c51abd1-4bb0-55f3-b5ed-d2efb9956354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{2x}+C}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350781} {:answer/id #uuid "3994077c-f43e-5d06-9d1e-d2342f644b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350782} {:answer/id #uuid "9829d042-e701-5f50-84b4-bcaa5269b3b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={({e}^{x/2}-2x+C)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3994077c-f43e-5d06-9d1e-d2342f644b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350782} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff790ca9-21da-56ed-8672-32ce810cbd5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00b0a77d-c476-5309-904d-0aecc5479d50" :question/math-academy-id "q-109029" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z=1\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z{e}^{-x/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-x/2} & =-{e}^{-x/2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =C{e}^{x/2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2}=C{e}^{x/2}-1\\,⇒\\,y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66be850d-8650-5de7-85a5-9cd4ddb3c8b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}+C}{3{x}^{1/4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350786} {:answer/id #uuid "dc27e877-245e-5e4a-876f-f2076a6d2fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{C{x}^{1/2}-2}{4{x}^{1/2}})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350787} {:answer/id #uuid "779d22d4-32ad-50cf-bc93-c67e8e96e9ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{5/4}+C}{5{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350788} {:answer/id #uuid "046ef0da-6f80-5866-a017-efdfd5af782b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350789} {:answer/id #uuid "78fd1a01-9630-5403-abfd-3b00bab29c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{1/4}+C}{4{x}^{9/4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "046ef0da-6f80-5866-a017-efdfd5af782b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350789} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f64f63e-4fa1-560f-b209-4c5259aa8148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f7b3833-fb2f-501a-9c17-6d0b0eb8fbd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109170" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\nx\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{3/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3/4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{1/4}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-3/4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{4x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/4x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{1/4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ {x}^{1/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/4}z & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4}) & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{1/4} & =\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{9/4}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=9{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/4}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/4}=\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}}\\,⇒\\,y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}\\,.\n$$"}]}
